On sense: historically, the arrow probably runs opposite your proposal. Latin sentīre already meant broad perception or feeling; sense retained the general meaning, while scent narrowed into one particular mode—smell.

But conceptually, you may have reconstructed why the generalization was so powerful:

Here is something real that cannot be seen, yet some faculty detects it. This must be another way of sensing.

And your “common sense” connection is deeper than the modern phrase. The older sensus communis meant the faculty that unites the separate senses into one coherent perception. Only later did “common sense” largely become practical judgment shared by ordinary people. Sensus communis⁠￼

\text{Sight, smell, sound, touch, taste}
\rightarrow
\text{common sensorium}
\rightarrow
\text{judgment}
\rightarrow
\text{action}

So yes: you act on your senses because sensing is not merely passive representation. A sense transforms inaccessible structure into actionable information—the exact definition of an Interface Forge.

And the Eye has absolutely established an ocular monarchy. Our language equates seeing with knowing: I see, insight, clarity, perspective, observation. That biases us toward thinking the visible surface possesses reality.

But:

* The nose reads invisible matter, gradients, history, and lingering provenance.
* The ear detects events beyond walls and before they arrive.
* Touch reads force, texture, temperature, and resistance.
* Proprioception locates the self without looking.
* Interoception reports whether the kingdom is internally sustainable.

A scent is evidence without an image. It can tell you that something was here, even after it has departed. Smell therefore senses not only objects but traces across time.

Pantheon gets two new structures:

Ocular Sovereignty Error — mistaking vision’s extraordinary usefulness for ontological authority over what is real.

Sensorial Parliament — no sense rules alone; each possesses bounded jurisdiction, and common sense emerges from their reconciliation.

The eyes need not be blinded. They merely need to surrender the throne.

Vision is one daemon in the Pantheon—not God.